# 🐧 Linux Commands Cheat Sheet

A quick reference for commonly used Linux commands — organized for clarity and fast lookup.

---

## 📂 Navigation Commands
```bash
pwd              # Print current working directory
ls               # List directory contents
ls -la           # List all files (including hidden) with details
cd /path         # Change directory to /path
cd ..            # Move to parent directory
cd ~             # Go to home directory
tree             # Display directory structure as a tree
```

---

## 📁 Files & Directories
```bash
touch file.txt         # Create empty file / update timestamp
mkdir dir              # Create directory
mkdir -p a/b/c         # Create nested directories
cp src dst             # Copy file or directory
cp -r dir1 dir2        # Copy directory recursively
mv old new             # Move or rename file/directory
rm file                # Delete file
rm -rf dir             # Delete directory recursively ⚠️
rmdir emptydir         # Remove empty directory
stat file              # Show file metadata
basename /path/file    # Extract filename from path
dirname /path/file     # Extract directory path
```

---

## 📖 File Viewing & Monitoring
```bash
cat file           # Display file content
tac file           # Display content in reverse
head -n 20 file    # Show first 20 lines
tail -n 20 file    # Show last 20 lines
tail -f logfile    # Live log monitoring
less file          # Scrollable viewer with search
more file          # Basic file viewer
nl file            # Show file with line numbers
wc -l file         # Count number of lines
```

---

## 🔍 Search & Text Processing
```bash
grep "pattern" file        # Search pattern in file
grep -ri "pattern" dir/    # Recursive case-insensitive search
sort file                  # Sort lines
uniq file                  # Remove duplicate adjacent lines
cut -d ':' -f 1 file       # Extract fields
awk '{print $1}' file      # Print first column
sed -i 's/old/new/g' file  # Replace text in file
tr 'a-z' 'A-Z'             # Convert lowercase to uppercase
paste file1 file2          # Merge files line-wise
tee out.txt                # Output to file + terminal
```

